---
title: 'How to Answer Job Application Questions: Why Us, Why You and Salary Expectations (With Examples)'
publishedAt: 2026-11-11
type: job-seekers
---
**Quick answer:** Answer application questions in three to five sentences. Lead with one specific reason or fact, back it with an example from your work, and connect it to something in the job post. For "Why do you want to work here?", name something real about the company's product, stage or problem. For "Why are you a good fit?", match two requirements from the post to two results you've delivered. For salary expectations, give a researched range rather than one number. Generic answers are the easiest way to look like you applied to fifty jobs in an hour.

More companies now add short questions to their application forms, and recruiters read them. On many forms, the answers are the first thing a person looks at, before the resume. A good answer won't win you the job by itself, but a lazy one can lose it.

## Why do companies ask application questions?

Application questions do three jobs for a hiring team:

1. **They show effort.** Anyone can click "Easy Apply". A thoughtful answer shows you read the post.
2. **They check must-haves.** Questions like "Are you authorized to work in India?" or "Can you start within 30 days?" filter for hard requirements.
3. **They show how you communicate.** For remote and async teams, written communication is part of the job.

On GetHired, employers can grade answers as part of AI-assisted screening, and short or generic answers are marked weak or missing. Recruiters at other companies do the same thing by eye.

## The answer formula that works for almost every question

Use this structure for any open-ended question:

1. **Direct answer** in one sentence.
2. **Evidence**: a specific example, number or project.
3. **Connection** to the role or company.

Keep it to 50 to 150 words unless the form asks for more. Recruiters skim.

## How to answer "Why do you want to work here?"

This is the most common application question, and the one most people answer badly. "I'm passionate about your mission and would love to grow with a dynamic team" says nothing.

**What to include:**

- Something specific about the company: the product, a recent launch, the technical problem, the customers or the stage.
- Why that matters to you, based on your experience.
- What you'd bring to it.

**Weak answer:**

> I've always admired your company and I'm excited about the opportunity to work in a fast-paced environment where I can grow my skills.

**Strong answer:**

> I've used your API for a side project that syncs invoices between two accounting tools, and the webhook retry logic saved me hours of work. I'd like to work on that kind of developer-facing infrastructure full time. At my current job I rebuilt our payment webhook handler to cut failed deliveries by 60%, so the reliability problems in this role are ones I've already worked on.

The strong answer is specific, shows real contact with the product, and ties back to a result.

**If you don't know much about the company**, spend ten minutes on their website, blog, changelog, or recent funding news. If you can't find one thing that interests you, that's useful information too.

## How to answer "Why are you a good fit for this role?"

Pick the two or three most important requirements in the job post and match each one with proof.

**Template:**

> The role asks for [requirement 1] and [requirement 2]. At [company], I [result that shows requirement 1]. I've also [result that shows requirement 2]. I'd bring the same approach to [something specific in the role].

**Example:**

> The role asks for production experience with Go and Postgres at scale. At Acme, I built and ran our order service in Go, handling about 3,000 requests per second, and cut p95 latency from 400 ms to 90 ms by fixing N+1 queries and adding read replicas. I've also mentored two junior engineers through their first on-call rotations, which matches the mentoring part of this role.

## How to answer "Tell us about a project you're proud of"

Choose a project that's relevant to the job, then cover:

- **The problem** in one sentence.
- **Your part** (say "I", not only "we").
- **The hard part** and how you solved it.
- **The result**, with a number if you have one.

If you link to a repository or demo, make sure it works and has a short README. Our [guide to building a portfolio](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/the-definitive-guide-to-crafting-a-standout-portfolio) covers what reviewers look for.

## How to answer "What are your salary expectations?"

You have three reasonable options:

{% table %}
- Approach
- When to use it
- Example
---
- Give a researched range
- Most of the time
- "Based on the role and market data, I'm looking for ₹28–34 LPA base, depending on the full package."
---
- Ask for their range first
- Early stage or a free-text field
- "I'd like to understand the full package. Could you share the budgeted range for this role?"
---
- Give your minimum honestly
- When the field requires one number
- Enter your realistic minimum, not a hopeful maximum
{% /table %}

Tips:

- Research using job posts that publish pay, salary surveys, and people in similar roles.
- Make the bottom of your range a number you'd genuinely accept.
- Mention that you're open to discussing the full package (equity, bonus, benefits). Our [offer negotiation guide](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-negotiate-a-tech-job-offer-in-2026-salary-equity-and-scripts-that-work) covers the next step.

If the job post already lists a range and it works for you, say so: "The posted range of $120k–$140k works for me."

## How to answer yes/no and knockout questions

Questions like "Do you have the right to work in Germany?" or "Can you work from our Bengaluru office three days a week?" are often **knockout questions**. A "no" may move your application to rejected automatically.

- **Answer honestly.** Wrong answers come out in interviews or background checks, and they end offers.
- **Read carefully.** "Will you now or in the future require visa sponsorship?" asks about the future too.
- **Use the comment box** if there is one, to add context: "I don't currently have a work permit but I'm eligible for the EU Blue Card."

## How to answer "Is there anything else you'd like us to know?"

This optional box is a chance, not a trap. Use it for:

- A short note about a career change or [employment gap](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-explain-an-employment-gap-on-your-resume-and-in-interviews).
- A relevant project that isn't on your resume.
- Your notice period or availability.

Skip it if you have nothing new to say. Don't repeat your resume.

## Can I use AI to write application answers?

Yes, as long as the answer is true and sounds like you. AI tools are good at turning your notes into clean sentences. They're bad at knowing what you actually did.

Rules that keep you safe:

- **Start from facts you supply**: your resume, the job post and a few notes.
- **Edit every answer.** Remove phrases like "dynamic", "leverage", "passionate about synergy".
- **Check every claim.** If it says you led a team of eight and you didn't, delete it.

The [GetHired Chrome extension](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/extension) fills application forms on company career sites and writes plain-text answers to open questions based only on your resume, profile and the job. It never invents links or details, and you review and submit the application yourself.

## A quick checklist before you hit submit

- Did you answer the question that was asked?
- Does every answer include at least one specific detail?
- Did you mention the company or role by name where it fits?
- Is it under 150 words unless a longer answer was requested?
- Did you check spelling, especially of the company name?
- Are your yes/no answers honest?

## Frequently asked questions

### How long should answers to application questions be?

Usually 50 to 150 words, or three to five sentences. Follow any word limit on the form. Longer isn't better if the extra words are generic.

### Should I reuse the same answers for every job?

Reuse your evidence (projects, results, numbers), but rewrite the first and last sentences for each company. Recruiters recognise copy-pasted answers quickly.

### What if a question is optional?

Answer it if you have something useful to say. Optional questions are often where strong candidates stand out.

### Is it okay to say I don't know the salary range?

It's fine to ask for theirs first, but come prepared with your own range. Saying "anything is fine" can lead to a lower offer.

### Do recruiters actually read application answers?

Many do, especially at startups and smaller companies, and AI screening tools increasingly grade answers too. Treat every answer as if a hiring manager will read it.

